Verified against pixels and the served DOM (`client/dist-hydrated/demo/tx/0xb636167a05b9de55bde67756bdaed6e766d46359/debug/index.html`): exit `← demo` → `/demo`; the focused truncated hash `0xb63616…6359`, ring x81-197 y17-45, glyphs #101010 on #FFFFFF at 19.03:1; eight stepping chips; `128 / 1,315`; `Engine loading — 18 MB`; live `FETCHING` rail; `DATA` + `Synthetic demo data` as the transaction pane's labelled row with the producer's prose beneath; no band above the bar; no tick, toast, failure banner or landing notice.

No layout shift — proved, not assumed. This frame differs from the plain-build `debugger--loading-phases` capture by exactly **568 pixels**, every one #FFFFFF→#3B82F6, bounded exactly at x81-197 y17-45. That ring is the entire visible output of upgrading 39 elements into buttons, and the panes are pixel-identical to the plain build.

Population question, answered: **no** — at rest the two classes look the same, because both draw nothing. They diverge only in states this image cannot hold (hover surface, `cursor:copy`) and by 4px of corner radius under focus. The framing is off; the defect is the shared zero, not the difference. And the divergence runs backwards: the two truncated identifiers, where the button is the only way to get the value, are the pair with no pointer cue at all.

Highest-priority fixes: two declarations (`.copybtn{cursor:copy;border-radius:var(--bt-radius-xs)}` + a `:hover`) to unify the populations; then draw `.copied`/`.copyfailed`, the one state where a pixel is owed and costs nothing at rest.

